  Version 0.3.2 (2026-11-03)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Copy only tracked recipe inputs and retain the shared copier in source exports.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.3.1 (2026-11-02)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 350 (Daniel, 2026-11-02: "Why machinery to keep a failure for each?"): each problem is kept by the
    edition's one engine, sparky-stereo-problems, for this boot, only when it changes, and shown by its
    session notifier; the package's own store and user units go. The status (PROTECTION, PROBLEMS, REASON)
    and --problems read the engine's records; --notify starts the session's notifier. The messages, word for
    word, are built into journal catalogs; the problems go when the package is removed

  * Nothing fails silently (Daniel, 2026-11-01; ORDER 346b, rows U17, U18 and U54 of ORDER 343's audit)
  * Whatever keeps the daemon from protecting memory, or from protecting it the way it was set, is a problem kept in /run/cushion-swap/problems, said once in the journal and shown once per session as a notification, in the nine languages
  * The status names it: PROTECTION (yes, degraded, no), PROBLEMS, REASON, MODE_REASON, CALIBRATION, STARTED and PID
  * No swap area, an area that is not there, an unreadable /proc/meminfo or /proc/swaps and a failed swapon say that memory is not protected; a failed swapoff says the area stays on
  * --status exits 0 only while memory is protected: 1 with a problem kept, 2 not protecting, 3 not running, 4 unreadable; --problems lists them in the system's language
  * The unit keeps its status past the daemon (RuntimeDirectoryPreserve=yes), says why it stopped (ExecStopPost) and gives systemctl status a status line (NotifyAccess=all)
  * The package checks that the restarted daemon runs and says whether it protects memory; each failed setup step is said with its own error, and the package still configures
  * PSI: a kernel without it, or PSI=off, is a mode; a kernel with PSI whose helper is missing or whose trigger is refused or lost is a problem; the poller keeps watching as before, and PSI=off is offered as a choice
  * Calibration: a missing helper, a failed fingerprint, a failed measurement (tried again a minute later, as before) or a result that cannot be kept are problems; the conservative pace stays, with MAX_ALLOC_MIB_S offered as a choice
  * Hibernation: a failed switch-on, a kernel resume device that differs and a failed switch-off after resume fail their command and are kept

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.3.0 (2026-10-11)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * Daniel's refinements of 2026-10-10
  * Headroom pacing scales to the machine's own allocation rate, measured once while idle and cached by kernel, CPU, RAM and firmware identity
  * The default log has only real problems: failures, and DEPLOY with its RETRACT and their arithmetic; every-cycle logging is LOG_EVERY_CYCLE=yes in /etc/default/cushion-swap
  * Rotation weekly, one week kept, no size limit and no hourly timer
  * The test runner checks for logrotate before it starts

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.2.0 (2026-10-10)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * Daniel's nine decisions of 2026-10-10
  * Headroom pacing by default; the fixed rate stays as PACING=fixed; a game opening still caps the interval
  * Thresholds by RAM below 90 GiB of MemTotal, decided; his values from 90 GiB up
  * An area that is on and holds nothing is retracted after the quiet window; never while a hibernation uses it
  * Every cycle logged with its arithmetic to /var/log/cushion-swap/cushion-swap.log, rotated daily by logrotate
  * DEPLOY, RETRACT, failures and the startup lines in the journal as well
  * Hibernate to the device named in /etc/initramfs-tools/conf.d/resume (RESUME=, RESUME_OFFSET=), else resume= on the
    kernel command line; a swap partition outside fstab works; the generator logs which source decided
  * An initramfs hook carries RESUME_OFFSET into the image (initramfs-tools reads only RESUME)
  * cushion-swap --resume-target says where the image goes and why

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.0 (2026-10-10)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  * First release: Daniel Ramos's swap parachute as the Cushion Swap File
  * The swap area stays off at boot; deployed under memory pressure, retracted when safe
  * PSI trigger wake-up where the kernel has it, his 3 s poller otherwise
  * Thresholds scale with RAM below 90 GiB of MemTotal; his values above
  * Hibernate offered and working with the area off at boot
  * Where Daniel's own cgm-swap-parachute.service is installed, nothing starts and Hibernate is left as it was
